    <%# The first panel on this page that MATCHES A TEST TO ITSELF across runs. Everything above it
        is either bounded to one run on purpose — the ranking, the by-file and by-directory
        rollups, the outcome counters, because "which tests were slow in this run" is a question
        one run's rows answer — or, in the "Areas that grew or shrank" panel directly above,
        spans two runs while pairing nothing inside them: it counts rows per area on each side and
        subtracts, and says in its own caption that it matches no tests. "Which tests changed their
        outcome" cannot be asked that way. It needs at least two runs AND a rule for deciding that
        a row in one of them and a row in another are the same test, which is why this panel states
        that rule below rather than assuming the reader shares it.

        Drawn on the SAME window as the "Suite growth" panel above — same `?branch=` anchor, same
        `Repository::TRAJECTORY_LIMIT`, and literally the same loaded runs (see
        `RepositoriesController#show`). Branch-anchored because outcomes compared across branches
        are outcomes of different code; sharing the window because every panel that fetched
        "the last thirty runs on this branch" for itself would be its own window, with no
        structural reason to keep agreeing.

        Rendered when the window has per-example rows AT ALL. A repository whose CI has never sent
        per-example detail has no cross-run test history to discuss, and this panel explaining that
        at length would be discussing a table it has no rows in — the same line the three
        single-run panels above draw with their own `recorded?`. Outside the `manage_keys` gate for
        the same reason they are: read-only suite telemetry, nothing credential and nothing
        actionable. %>
    <% if @unstable_tests&.recorded? %>
      <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Tests whose outcome changed", id: "unstable-tests",
                                        class: "lg:col-span-3") do %>
        <% if @unstable_tests.comparable? %>
          <%# What was compared, by what rule, and what the comparison could not reach — all before
              the list, because every one of them is a precondition for reading it rather than a
              note about it.

              The window figures are counted off the same runs the groups below were grouped over
              (`UnstableTests` holds both), so this paragraph cannot end up describing a different
              window from the one the rows came from. The matching rule is stated because it is the
              one DECISION on this panel rather than a measurement: everything else here was
              counted, but "the same description in two runs is the same test" is a rule the reader
              has to be able to check against their own repository. %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted mb-3" id="unstable-tests-basis">
            <%= unstable_tests_window_sentence(@unstable_tests) %>
            <%= unstable_tests_matching_sentence %>
            <%= unstable_tests_boundary_sentence %>
            <%# The two silences: rows the rule could not use, and candidates the narrowing did not
                reach. Rendered only when there are any — a clause reading "0 rows carried no
                description" is a sentence about arithmetic rather than about this window. %>
            <%= unstable_tests_exclusion_sentence(@unstable_tests) %>
          </p>
          <% if @unstable_tests.any? %>
            <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Test", "Runs seen", "Runs failed", "Outcomes seen"],
                                              describedby: "unstable-tests-basis") do %>
              <% @unstable_tests.rows.each do |test| %>
                <tr>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content">
                    <%# The way INTO the row, and until it existed this ranking dead-ended at the
                        one question it is read to answer. The row states that a test failed in 4 of
                        30 runs and shows the words those runs used; WHICH 4 decides whether the
                        reader hunts a commit or quarantines a test, and nothing anywhere said. The
                        aggregate above cannot say — it is `COUNT`s and `ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT …)` under
                        `GROUP BY name`, which is what keeps it constant in the size of the suite —
                        so the axis comes back from a rung below rather than from changing it.

                        `?unstable_test=`, following the `?spec_file=` / `?spec_directory=` /
                        `?repeated_description=` precedent on this same page rather than a nested
                        route: a description contains anything a person can type and makes a poor
                        route segment.

                        The name is the link TEXT rather than a separate control, exactly as the
                        description is one panel over and the file path two — the thing a reader
                        wants to open is the thing they are already looking at.

                        Every other ask is CARRIED THROUGH by `drill_down_path`: `?branch=` anchors
                        the "Suite growth" panel this window is drawn on, `?commit_sha=` names which
                        run the whole page describes, and the drill-in parameters anchor panels of
                        their own — so dropping any of them here would close a panel the reader did
                        not touch, or jump them back to the newest run, as the side effect of
                        opening a test.

                        `aria-current` on the open test, because this is a list of choices with one
                        of them taken and the panel below is a long way down the page — a reader
                        arriving back at this table by keyboard needs to be told which row they are
                        already looking at. %>
                    <%= link_to test.name,
                                drill_down_path(@repository, unstable_test: test.name,
                                                anchor: "unstable-test-runs"),
                                class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                                       "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
                                aria: { current: test.name == @unstable_test_request ? "true" : nil } %>
                    <%# A group spanning more than one spec file. Per the project's identity rule a
                        test that MOVED is the same test and keeps its history, so this is not an
                        error to flag — but a reader hunting a flaky test in one file needs to know
                        the history they are reading spans two. The files are named rather than
                        counted, because "recorded under 2 files" sends the reader looking without
                        saying where. %>
                    <% if test.multi_file? %>
                      <span class="block font-mono text-xs text-app-muted">
                        recorded under <%= test.files_seen.to_sentence %>
                      </span>
                    <% end %>
                  </td>
                  <%# Always the fraction. "12" alone reads as twelve of something unstated, and on
                      this panel the denominator IS the claim: a test seen in 12 of 30 runs is a
                      test whose history covers less than half the window. %>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                    <%= test.appearance_label(@unstable_tests.run_count) %>
                  </td>
                  <%# Over the runs it APPEARED in, never over the window: a test added halfway
                      through failed in two of the fifteen runs that ran it, and dividing by thirty
                      would report a stability it was never measured for. %>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content tabular-nums"><%= test.failure_label %></td>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2">
                    <%# The words the runs used, echoed verbatim and never folded into a verdict —
                        nothing platform-side validates this string, so quoting what arrived is the
                        only reading that cannot be wrong. Toned through
                        `SpecObservation.outcome_tone`, the same seam the single-run column above
                        renders through, so a word cannot be coloured one way in one run's list and
                        another way across a window. %>
                    <span class="flex flex-wrap gap-1">
                      <% test.outcome_words.each do |word| %>
                        <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: SpecObservation.outcome_tone(word)) do %>
                          <%= word %>
                        <% end %>
                      <% end %>
                      <%# Runs that recorded this test and said nothing about how it ended. Silence
                          inside a population that reported is not covered by the words beside it,
                          and it is not a pass — it wears `#outcome_label`'s own vocabulary and its
                          neutral tone for exactly that reason. %>
                      <% if test.unreported_outcome_count.positive? %>
                        <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :neutral) do %>
                          not reported ×<%= test.unreported_outcome_count %>
                        <% end %>
                      <% end %>
                    </span>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
          <% else %>
            <%# The honest zero, reachable only behind `comparable?` above — so it is only ever
                printed over a window that DID report outcomes, and never over one that was silent.
                Worded against the runs it was drawn from, and worded differently for each of the
                three different facts it can stand for: nothing failed at all, things failed and
                always failed, and something varied but under a description this panel cannot
                attribute to one example. See `RepositoriesHelper#unstable_tests_none_description`. %>
            <div id="unstable-tests-none">
              <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                    title: "No test changed its outcome in this window",
                    description: unstable_tests_none_description(@unstable_tests)
                  ) %>
            </div>
          <% end %>
          <%# The groups the rule could not rule on. Below the list rather than inside it, because
              they are not the same kind of finding: the list above says "this test's outcome
              changed", and about these the only honest statement is that something under one
              description did. %>
          <% if @unstable_tests.shared_description_rows.any? %>
            <div id="unstable-tests-shared" class="mt-4 border-t border-app-border-light pt-3">
              <p class="text-xs text-app-muted" id="unstable-tests-shared-basis">
                <%= unstable_tests_shared_description_sentence(@unstable_tests) %>
              </p>
              <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-app-content">
                <% @unstable_tests.shared_description_rows.each do |test| %>
                  <li>
                    <%= test.name %>
                    <span class="text-xs text-app-muted">
                      — <%= test.recorded_count %> examples under this description across
                      <%= test.appearance_label(@unstable_tests.run_count) %> runs
                    </span>
                  </li>
                <% end %>
              </ul>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        <% else %>
          <%# Fewer than two runs of the window reported an outcome, so there is no comparison to
              make. No zero and no empty list styled as a result: `outcome` is nullable and nothing
              validates it, so a window whose client sends none produces exactly the empty list a
              perfectly stable window does, and "0 unstable tests" printed over it would be
              "nobody told us" wearing the spelling of "everything is fine". That is Vacuous Green
              verbatim, and `SlowestExamples#outcomes_reported?` refuses it one grain down for a
              single run. %>
          <div id="unstable-tests-incomparable">
            <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                  title: "Not enough runs reported outcomes to compare",
                  description: unstable_tests_incomparable_description(@unstable_tests)
                ) %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
